.TH NOTI\-MAPPER.D 5 "2026" "noti-mapper 0.1.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
noti\-mapper.d \- configuration files for noti\-mapper
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
/etc/noti\-mapper.d/*.json
/etc/noti\-mapper/secrets.json
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Configuration is a set of JSON files in \fI/etc/noti\-mapper.d/\fR, read in
lexical order and merged. A later file may add instances and rules; redefining
a name an earlier file already defined is an error, not an override. Silent
shadowing across drop\-in files is miserable to debug and there is no use case
for it here.
.PP
Every file is a JSON object with one or more of the top\-level keys
.BR instances ,
.BR rules ,
and
.BR daemon .
A single file may carry all of them.
.PP
Run
.B noti\-mapper validate
after editing. Every problem is reported in one pass, with the file it is in
and the path through that file, for example
.IR "instances \-> 'Porch Mail' \-> plugin" .
.SH INSTANCES
An
.I instance
is a configured, named occurrence of a plugin. Two IMAP readers watching
different mailboxes are two instances of one plugin.
.PP
.RS
.nf
{
  "instances": {
    "Porch Mail": {
      "plugin": "imap-input",
      "config": {
        "host": "mail.example.net",
        "username": "user@example.net",
        "password": "${secret:porch_mail_password}",
        "folder": "Packages",
        "senders": ["amazon.com", "ups.com"],
        "subject_patterns": ["^Delivered:"],
        "dry_run": false
      }
    },
    "Porch Lamp": {
      "plugin": "homekit-output",
      "config": { "display_name": "Package Waiting" }
    }
  }
}
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B plugin
Required. The plugin's own identifier, not a name you choose. An unknown value
is a fatal error whose message lists the plugins that did load.
.TP
.B config
Optional. A plugin\-specific object. Each plugin validates its own, so
unrecognised keys and missing required ones are reported by
.BR "noti-mapper validate" .
.TP
.B enabled
Optional, default \fBtrue\fR. A disabled instance is not started and drops out
of every rule that names it.
.SH RULES
A
.I rule
maps a set of input instances to a set of output instances. The latch belongs
to the rule.
.PP
.RS
.nf
{
  "rules": {
    "Package On Porch": {
      "inputs": ["Porch Mail"],
      "outputs": ["Porch Lamp", "Porch Pager"]
    }
  }
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B Renaming a rule is not free.
The latch is keyed on the rule name, so changing that name here is
indistinguishable from deleting one rule and creating another: the old latch
orphans and the new rule starts cleared. Use
.B noti\-mapper rename
to migrate the latch first. See
.BR noti-mapper (1).
.TP
.B inputs
Required, non\-empty. An event from any listed instance sets the latch; inputs
are OR'd.
.TP
.B outputs
Required, non\-empty. Every listed instance is driven from the latch, and an
unlatch from any of them clears it.
.TP
.B enabled
Optional, default \fBtrue\fR.
.SH SEMANTICS
.SS Two outputs on one rule are coupled
Clearing from either output clears the rule, so both go quiet together. A user
who wants to clear the lamp without resolving the pager writes two rules with
one output each. The coupling is a configuration decision rather than a
hardcoded behaviour, which is the point of keying latches on the rule.
.SS The same input in two rules latches twice
Each rule gets its own latch. The input fires, both set, and clearing one
leaves the other set.
.SS An output is the OR of its rules
An output listed by two rules is true while either latch is set, and clearing
one rule does not drop it while the other is still set.
.SS Re\-triggering is not a no\-op
An event against an already\-set latch bumps a counter and moves the timestamp,
so an output can render "3 packages waiting", but it produces no output
transition.
.SH DAEMON
Optional, and at most one file may contain it.
.PP
.RS
.nf
{
  "daemon": {
    "event_log_max_rows": 10000,
    "dispatcher_threads": 4,
    "retry_initial_seconds": 5,
    "retry_max_seconds": 900
  }
}
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B event_log_max_rows
Rows kept in the rolling event log, oldest dropped first. Default 10000.
.TP
.B dispatcher_threads
Threads performing outbound pushes. Default 4.
.TP
.B retry_initial_seconds
Delay before the first retry of a failed push, doubling thereafter. Default 5.
.TP
.B retry_max_seconds
Ceiling on the retry delay. Default 900.
.SH SECRETS
Secrets live in \fI/etc/noti\-mapper/secrets.json\fR, deliberately outside the
\fB.d\fR glob so they are not swept up by configuration merging and can carry
different permissions. The file is a flat object mapping names to strings:
.PP
.RS
.nf
{ "porch_mail_password": "hunter2" }
.fi
.RE
.PP
Reference a secret from any string inside an instance's
.B config
block as \fB${secret:name}\fR. Substitution works inside a larger string, so
\fB"imaps://${secret:user}@host"\fR is fine.
.PP
The file must be mode 0600. The daemon refuses to start if it is readable by
group or other, and says so with the offending mode. A referenced secret that
does not exist is a configuration validation error, not a runtime failure.
.PP
The design goal is worth stating: configuration files never contain secret
material, which means they stay safe to paste into a bug report.
.SH VALIDATION
The daemon refuses to start on an unknown plugin, a duplicate name
(case\-insensitively), a rule referencing an instance that does not exist, a
rule listing an output instance among its inputs or the reverse, a missing
required plugin setting, a group\- or world\-readable secrets file, or malformed
JSON. All errors are reported together.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR noti-mapper (1)
